---
import DocShell from '../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'states', label: 'The four states' },
   { id: 'numbers', label: 'Coverage by level' },
   { id: 'strategy', label: 'Testing strategy' },
   { id: 'lookup', label: 'Looking a criterion up' },
   { id: 'claims', label: 'What a run proves' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Coverage"
   summary="Coverage says whether automation can decide a success criterion, and what evidence the criterion needs when it cannot. a11ied derives it from the W3C ACT rule mapping and the axe-core rule tags, both shipped as local data."
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section class="ledger" id="states">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">States</p>
         <h2>The four coverage states</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>automated</code>
            <span>
               One or more axe rules decide the criterion. <code>a1 axe --criterion</code> runs them
               and the result stands on its own.
            </span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>hybrid</code>
            <span>
               Automation gathers part of the evidence and a person judges the rest. Ten criteria in
               WCAG 2.2 land here, including Focus Order and Status Messages.
            </span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>manual</code>
            <span>
               No rule mapping exists. a11ied returns the criterion text, techniques, and failures,
               and a person makes the call.
            </span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>unknown</code>
            <span>
               The mapping data did not classify the criterion. No criterion in WCAG 2.1 or 2.2 is in
               this state today.
            </span>
         </div>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="split panel" id="numbers">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Numbers</p>
            <h2>Coverage by conformance level</h2>
         </div>
         <p>WCAG 2.2 has 86 success criteria. The pinned mapping classifies them like this:</p>
         <div class="route-list">
            <div class="route-row">
               <strong>Level A</strong>
               <span>31 criteria. 15 automated, 3 hybrid, 13 manual.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="route-row">
               <strong>Level AA</strong>
               <span>24 criteria. 8 automated, 4 hybrid, 12 manual.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="route-row">
               <strong>Level AAA</strong>
               <span>31 criteria. 5 automated, 3 hybrid, 23 manual.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="route-row">
               <strong>All levels</strong>
               <span>86 criteria. 28 automated, 10 hybrid, 48 manual.</span>
            </div>
         </div>
         <p>
            29 criteria have at least one axe rule. 24 have at least one ACT rule. 21 have both.
         </p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">Where it comes from</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            Every coverage record lists its <code>sourceAttribution</code>. The five values are
            act-mapping, axe-rule-tags, axe-act-id-join, strategy-heuristic, and manual-fallback.
         </p>
         <p class="aside-note">
            See <a href="/reference/wcag-data">WCAG data</a> for the upstream sources.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="strategy">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Strategy</p>
         <h2>What the criterion needs</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         Alongside coverage, every criterion carries a testing strategy. It names the procedures to
         run and says whether the test needs a rendered target.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 wcag coverage 4.1.3 --json</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>"coverage": &#123;
  "coverageState": "hybrid",
  "axeRuleIds": [],
  "actRuleIds": [],
  "sourceAttribution": ["strategy-heuristic"]
&#125;,
"strategy": &#123;
  "preferredEvidenceMode": "hybrid",
  "procedureIds": ["status_message_probe"],
  "requiresRealTarget": true,
  "notes": [
    "Status messages need announcement evidence from assistive technology without forced focus changes."
  ]
&#125;</code></pre>
      <p>
         <code>requiresRealTarget: true</code> means static markup is not enough. Run the page and
         move through it. For Status Messages that means triggering the update and reading the
         driver transcript to see whether the reader announced it.
      </p>
      <p>
         Criteria with <code>"procedureIds": ["axe_scan"]</code> need nothing beyond
         <code>a1 axe</code>. Criteria with <code>["manual_review"]</code> need a person.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="lookup">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Lookup</p>
         <h2>Finding a criterion</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         All five lookups read bundled data. None of them opens a browser or makes a network
         request.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 wcag levels --json
a1 wcag criteria --level AA --json
a1 wcag show 4.1.3 --json
a1 wcag search "focus order" --limit 3 --json
a1 wcag coverage 4.1.3 --json</code></pre>
      <p>
         <code>show</code> and <code>coverage</code> take an ID such as <code>4.1.3</code> or a slug
         such as <code>status-messages</code>. Use <code>search</code> when you know the concept but
         not the number. It ranks matches across titles, summaries, normative text, techniques,
         failures, and tags.
      </p>
      <p>
         Every <code>wcag</code> command takes <code>--version</code>. WCAG 2.1 and 2.2 both ship
         with the package, and 2.2 is the default.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note" id="claims">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Claims</p>
         <h2>Read coverage before you report a result</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         An axe run with no violations covers the 8 automated criteria at level AA. It says nothing
         about the other 16. Reporting that run as level AA conformance is wrong, and it is the
         mistake this data exists to stop.
      </p>
      <p>
         Report the coverage state next to the result. Name the criteria a run decided, name the
         ones a driver transcript supports, and name the ones still waiting on a person.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
